WEBVTT

00:03.880 --> 00:04.200
All right.

00:04.200 --> 00:09.000
So now we're going to summarize and talk quickly about how to use the right index.

00:09.000 --> 00:10.400
So when to use which type.

00:10.480 --> 00:11.800
Let's start with the first one.

00:11.800 --> 00:13.280
We have the heap structure.

00:13.280 --> 00:16.640
So as we learned it is a table without any index.

00:16.640 --> 00:22.480
So in which scenario we don't have to use any indexes in case you want to have fast inserts.

00:22.480 --> 00:27.400
So if you want to have a fast right performance then don't take any index.

00:27.400 --> 00:30.600
So you stay with the default with the heap structure of your table.

00:30.600 --> 00:36.480
And we usually use it in not very important tables, like the staging tables or temporary tables where

00:36.480 --> 00:40.880
we want to insert the data fast and then get rid of the data later.

00:40.880 --> 00:43.640
So here there is no need to utilize any index.

00:43.680 --> 00:49.640
Now, if you are talking about the clustered index, we usually use the clustered index for primary

00:49.640 --> 00:50.120
keys.

00:50.160 --> 00:52.000
It is even a default from the database.

00:52.000 --> 00:56.560
If you create any primary keys, then SQL going to go and create a clustered index.

00:56.560 --> 00:59.400
So this is the main usage of the clustered index.

00:59.400 --> 01:00.840
You use it in the primary keys.

01:00.880 --> 01:06.200
And if there is like no primary key in your table then you can go and pick another column where sorting

01:06.200 --> 01:09.680
the data is important, like for example a date column.

01:09.680 --> 01:12.370
So it could be a good candidate for your clustered index.

01:12.410 --> 01:14.090
Now moving on to another type.

01:14.090 --> 01:15.970
We have the Columnstore index.

01:16.130 --> 01:20.530
So when I say here clustered index I mean clustered row store index of course.

01:20.610 --> 01:23.810
Now the question is when do we use the Columnstore index.

01:23.890 --> 01:29.290
If you have like big complex analytical queries where you are aggregating a lot of data, doing data

01:29.290 --> 01:34.570
aggregations, then go for the Columnstore index because it's going to give you amazing performance.

01:34.570 --> 01:37.730
And as well if you are struggling with the size of tables.

01:37.730 --> 01:42.690
So if you have a super large table, you can go and use the Columnstore index because it can go and

01:42.690 --> 01:46.410
compress the data and reduce the size of the whole table.

01:46.530 --> 01:49.250
So for those scenarios we use the Columnstore index.

01:49.370 --> 01:55.570
So again for the row store clustered index we use it usually for the OLTP systems where you have a lot

01:55.610 --> 01:57.330
of transactions and so on.

01:57.330 --> 02:02.770
But for the Columnstore we use it usually for the OLAp systems where you have a data warehouse reporting

02:02.770 --> 02:04.490
system, business intelligence, and so on.

02:04.530 --> 02:07.930
Now moving on to another type we have the non-clustered index.

02:07.970 --> 02:11.770
We usually use this index for non-primary key columns.

02:11.770 --> 02:16.570
So that means the rest of the columns of your tables could be candidates for the non-clustered index.

02:16.570 --> 02:19.170
And there are a lot of reasons why you would do that.

02:19.170 --> 02:25.740
For example, for the foreign keys or using it on the columns that are used in order to join two tables,

02:25.740 --> 02:31.140
and another place where you can use the non-clustered index for the columns that are used for the Where

02:31.180 --> 02:31.780
clause.

02:31.820 --> 02:36.620
So there are like many scenarios where we can use the non-clustered index, but not for the primary

02:36.620 --> 02:37.100
keys.

02:37.140 --> 02:40.100
Now moving on to another type we have the filtered index.

02:40.140 --> 02:43.420
We use it in order to target a subset of data.

02:43.420 --> 02:49.380
So if in our query and analysis we are only focusing on a subset of data all time, it makes no sense

02:49.380 --> 02:51.900
to have one big index for all data.

02:51.900 --> 02:55.660
We can use the filtered index to have focused index.

02:55.660 --> 03:01.220
And of course, if the size of the index is a problem, then you can use a filtered index in order to

03:01.220 --> 03:04.420
reduce the overall size of the storage of the index.

03:04.460 --> 03:07.020
And then to the last type, we have the unique index.

03:07.020 --> 03:11.780
You can go and use the unique index in order to ensure data integrity of your table.

03:11.780 --> 03:15.060
And as well it might prove slightly the performance of your query.

03:15.100 --> 03:21.260
And thus because SQL has less tasks to do if the index is unique, once SQL finds a match, it's going

03:21.300 --> 03:22.540
to skip the search.

03:22.540 --> 03:26.740
So this is a quick summary and guide on when to use which index type.

03:26.780 --> 03:29.980
That usually helps me finding the right index.
